      "key": {
        "uuid": "0e00deb6_9d04dea7",
        "filename": "src/Protocols/NLM/nsm.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 853,
      "author": {
        "id": 1038859
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-18T14:10:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. All fields (mon.mon_id.my_id.my_name, mon.mon_id.mon_name, client_name, client_address_str, server_address_str) plus the struct itself are now uniformly MEM_COMP_RECOVERY, matching both allocation origins: fill_forward_sm_notify() (live SM_NOTIFY forwarding) and parse_nlm_entry()/parse_cli_srv_netid_client() (recovery-file replay in nfs4_recovery.c). Previously they were split across MEM_COMP_PROTOCOL and MEM_COMP_FILE_AND_STATE_LOCK with no consistent rationale.\n\n2. mon.mon_id.my_id.my_name and mon.mon_id.mon_name are never actually populated on this struct anywhere — they stay NULL from gsh_calloc() for the lifetime of every local_nlm_info, so those two gsh_free() calls are no-ops today (freeing NULL is safe). Left them in the uniform free routine for defensive correctness in case that ever changes, rather than special-casing them out.\n\n3. client_name\u0027s component is now correct, but while auditing it I found the underlying pointer is not always safe to free even once: fill_forward_sm_notify() aliases the raw XDR-decoded string instead of copying it, so a single incoming SM_NOTIFY forwarded to N configured Cluster_Members ends up creating N local_nlm_info structs that all point at the same buffer, each of which calls local_nlm_info_free() independently. That\u0027s a real double-free/multi-free with 2+ cluster members.",
      "parentUuid": "5caa7afb_a3926561",
      "revId": "22be5b47831bbefda6c4c9dd9bc902a04c1996ca",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
